Given a sequence and two sequences of integers, determine if the given sequence can be split into two subsequences that each form a valid decreasing or increasing sequence respectively.

Let `s` be the given sequence, and let `decreasing` and `increasing` be the two target subsequences. We want to check if `s` can be split at some position `i` such that the first subsequence from `0` to `i` is decreasing and the second subsequence from `i+1` to the end of the sequence forms an increasing sequence.

We use dynamic programming to solve this problem. Let `dp[i][j]` be a boolean array where `dp[i][j]` is `true` if it's possible to form the first `i` elements of the decreasing/increasing subsequence ending at position `j`. 

Here's the Java code:

```java
public class CalculateSpans {

    public static int calculateSpans(String sequence, String[] sequences) {
        for (String s : sequences) {
            if (sequence.equals(s)) {
                return 1;
            }
        }
        return 0;
    }

    public static int findSequenceSplitIndex(String seq, String targetSeq) {
        int n = seq.length();
        boolean[] dpDecreasing = new boolean[n + 1];
        boolean[] dpIncreasing = new boolean[n + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if ((i == 0 || seq.charAt(i - 1) > seq.charAt(i)) && dpIncreasing[i]) {
                dpDecreasing[i + 1] = true;
            }
            if ((i == 0 || seq.charAt(i - 1) < seq.charAt(i)) && dpDecreasing[i]) {
                dpIncreasing[i + 1] = true;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (dpDecreasing[i] && dpIncreasing[n - i]) {
                return i;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static int calculateSpans(String sequence, String[] sequences) {
        for (String s : sequences) {
            if (sequence.equals(s)) {
                return 1;
            }
        }
        return 0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String testCases = "DecreasingSequence";
        int[] expectedResults = {1, 2, 3};

        System.out.println(calculateSpans(testCases, new String[]{"IncreasingSequence", "DecreasingSequence"})); // 2
        System.out.println(calculateSpans(testCases, new String[]{"IncreasingSequence", "IncreasingSequence"})); // 0
    }
}
```

This code uses two boolean arrays `dpDecreasing` and `dpIncreasing` to track if the first `i` elements of the decreasing/increasing subsequence ending at position `j` can be formed. It iterates through the sequence, updates these arrays based on whether the current element is greater or less than the previous one, and checks for any split point that satisfies both conditions.

Note that this code assumes that the input sequences are valid (i.e., they do not contain duplicate elements).
